.TH "XmeTraitGet" "library call"
.nh
.SH "NAME"
\fB\%XmeTraitGet\fP,
\fB\%XmeTraitSet\fP \(em Widget writer functions that look up and install traits
.iX "XmeTraitGet"
.iX "Xme trait functions" "XmeTraitGet"
.iX "Xme trait functions" "XmeTraitSet"
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.nf
#include <Xm/TraitP\&.h>
.PP
\fBXtPointer \fBXmeTraitGet\fP\fR(
\fBXtPointer \fBobject\fR\fR,
\fBXrmQuark \fBtrait\fR\fR);
.PP
\fBBoolean \fBXmeTraitSet\fP\fR(
\fBXtPointer \fBobject\fR\fR,
\fBXrmQuark \fBtrait\fR\fR,
\fBXtPointer \fBrecord\fR\fR);
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
A trait is a record of procedures, and sometimes data, through which
widgets that do not know each other cooperate: a widget that holds the
\fB\%XmQTactivatable\fP trait, for instance, can be the default button
of a
dialog\&.  The traits of Motif and their records are listed in
\fB\%Traits\fP(5)\&.
.PP
\fB\%XmeTraitSet\fP installs \fIrecord\fP as the \fItrait\fP of
\fIobject\fP, usually
a widget class, from its class_initialize or class_part_initialize
method; a NULL \fIrecord\fP removes the trait\&.  \fB\%XmeTraitGet\fP
returns
the record of \fItrait\fP that \fIobject\fP holds\&.
.PP
A trait is looked up on \fIobject\fP only, not on its superclasses\&.  A
class that wants its subclasses to hold its trait installs it again on
each of them from its class_part_initialize method, which Xt calls for
every subclass; a subclass can then install its own record instead\&.
The record is not copied and must stay valid as long as it is
installed\&.
.IP "\fIobject\fP" 10
Specifies the object, usually a widget class
(\fB\%XtClass\fP(\fIw\fP))\&.
.IP "\fItrait\fP" 10
Specifies the trait, for instance \fB\%XmQTactivatable\fP\&.
.IP "\fIrecord\fP" 10
Specifies the trait record, or NULL\&.
.SH "RETURN"
.PP
\fB\%XmeTraitGet\fP returns the record, or NULL if \fIobject\fP does not
hold
the trait\&.  \fB\%XmeTraitSet\fP returns True\&.
.SH "RELATED"
.PP
\fB\%Traits\fP(5)\&.
